---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { sections } from '../../../site.config';
import {
  getPublishedIntro,
  formatDate,
  slugFromId,
} from '../../../lib/content';

const articles = await getPublishedIntro();
---

<BaseLayout lang="en" title={sections.intro.labelEn}>
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="page-header__eyebrow">{sections.intro.labelEn}</p>
    <h1 class="page-header__title">{sections.intro.labelEn}</h1>
    <p class="page-header__desc">Welcome to Vincentian Topology.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      We read all the time, yet when it comes to speaking up or putting words on
      the page, the mind often goes blank.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read a lot but can't say what you think? That's not a personal failing. We
      get used to storing material—and forget to connect the circuit.
    </p>
    <p>
      At Vincentian Topology, we don't only share views. We also share how we
      work with information—the same process behind every piece on this site. We
      call it <em>ideas in collision</em>.
    </p>

    <p>Three simple ideas are enough:</p>

    <h3>1. Drop the weight: you think; AI polishes</h3>
    <p>
      Many people fear writing because they think they're "not good with words."
      But writing has two parts:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Thinking</strong>: finding material, asking questions, making
        judgments. That's yours to keep.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drafting</strong>: rhetoric, layout, polish. That's where tools
        shine.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Here, we hand polish to AI so you can focus on what matters:
      <strong>your view</strong>. If you have something to say, AI can help you
      say it clearly.
    </p>

    <h3>2. A simple process: from material to view</h3>
    <p>
      Once you have an idea, how do you make it stand? We skip heavy theory and
      use three steps:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Evidence</strong>: ground it in facts, not imagination.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Digest</strong>: connect material to life; decide what to keep
        and what to drop.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>View</strong>: land on a judgment you're willing to sign your
        name to.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>That's the path from reading others to speaking for yourself.</p>

    <h3>3. The goal: ideas in collision</h3>
    <p>
      <em>Ideas in collision</em> means bringing a question to the material until
      a view of your own emerges.
    </p>
    <p>
      It's not about winning an argument. It's about seeing how thought pulls
      between two poles. We hope what you read here isn't only a conclusion—but
      how that conclusion was reached.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h3>Ready to explore?</h3>
    <p>
      That's how Vincentian Topology works. Once you know the logic, you can start
      here:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        👉 <strong><a href="/en/view">Vincentian View</a></strong>: a quick take
        on what's happening now.
      </li>
      <li>
        👉 <strong><a href="/en/topics">Special Topics</a></strong>: one theme,
        explored in depth.
      </li>
      <li>
        👉 <strong><a href="/en/classics">Classic Commentary</a></strong>: slow
        reads on texts worth returning to.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>Or read the full introductory series below:</p>
  </div>

  <ul class="article-list">
    {
      articles.map((entry) => (
        <li class="article-list__item">
          <a
            class="article-list__link"
            href={`/en/intro/${slugFromId(entry.id)}`}
          >
            <span class="article-list__meta">
              0-{entry.data.order} · {formatDate(entry.data.published)}
            </span>
            <h2 class="article-list__title">{entry.data.title}</h2>
            {entry.data.description && (
              <p class="card__desc">{entry.data.description}</p>
            )}
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
